      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139067,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 224,
        "headingId": 734,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:04:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 970,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 735,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139068,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 224,
        "headingId": 735,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>9A06.4 Xanthelasma of eyelid</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:04:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 971,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 736,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139069,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 224,
        "headingId": 736,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable</em>: xanthomatous lesions.</p>\r\n<p><em>Not recommended</em>: histiocytomas; non&ndash;Langerhans cell histiocytosis.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:04:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 972,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 737,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139070,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 224,
        "headingId": 737,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Xanthoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:04:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 973,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 738,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139071,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 224,
        "headingId": 738,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Xanthelasma involves the eyelids.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:04:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 974,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 739,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139072,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 224,
        "headingId": 739,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Xanthelasma appears as flat or minimally elevated yellow lesions commonly in the medial aspect of the eyelids; the lesions are often bilateral and symmetrical {29296091}.</p>\r\n<p>A tuberous xanthoma is an elevated and nodular xanthelasma. These appear as solitary or multiple, placoid or papular lesions of the eyelids, more probably associated with hyperlipidaemia types&nbsp;II and&nbsp;III {23882283}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:04:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 975,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 740,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139073,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 224,
        "headingId": 740,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Xanthelasma has a reported incidence of 0.56&ndash;1.5% and represents approximately 6% of eyelid lesions {19384066}. The age of onset varies from 15&nbsp;to 75&nbsp;years with a peak between the fourth and fifth decades of life {23105704}. It is unclear if there is a female predilection.</p>\r\n<p>Xanthomas may occur in individuals of any age and show no sex predilection. In xanthoma disseminatum, there is a male predilection and the age of onset is &lt;&nbsp;25&nbsp;years in as many as a third of cases {19384066}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:04:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 976,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 741,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139074,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 224,
        "headingId": 741,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The etiology is unknown. Xanthelasma and xanthoma are not true neoplasms but are reactive developmental or inflammatory lesions {26794218}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:04:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 977,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 742,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139075,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 224,
        "headingId": 742,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The pathogenesis is unknown. The inflammatory milieu in xanthelasma is considered analogous to the early stages of cardiac atherosclerotic plaque formation {28481769}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:04:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 984,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 749,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139076,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 224,
        "headingId": 749,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>See <em>Clinical features</em>.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:04:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 978,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 743,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139077,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 224,
        "headingId": 743,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Xanthelasma shows focal collections of lipid-laden histiocytes distributed mainly around blood vessels and adnexal structures in the eyelid dermis with chronic inflammatory cells and fibrosis.</p>\r\n<p>Tuberous xanthomas are more deeply located in the dermis and display foamy histiocytes, Touton giant cells, extracellular cholesterol deposits, chronic inflammation, and fibrosis.</p>\r\n<h5>Immunohistochemistry</h5>\r\n<p>The foamy histiocytes typically express CD68, CD163, and factor&nbsp;XIIIa, but lack CD21, CD35, S100, CD1a, and Birbeck granules {33869889;19961259}.</p>\r\n<h5>Differential diagnosis</h5>\r\n<p>This includes necrobiotic xanthogranuloma, Erdheim&ndash;Chester disease and adult-onset xanthogranuloma with periocular xanthogranuloma {2043074;26966089}, Langerhans cell histiocytosis, Rosai&ndash;Dorfman disease, and crystal-storing histiocytosis (see related sections).</p>\r\n<p>A clinicopathological correlation may be necessary for challenging cases.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:04:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
